> ### Connection Between Infinitely Connected Graphs and Ising Model
> 
> 1. **Percolation Theory:**
>    - In percolation theory, a common example is the behavior of a connected graph or lattice as the probability of connection (or occupation) increases. 
>    - At low connection probabilities, isolated clusters exist with low likelihood of infinite clusters (or chunks). As the threshold probability is crossed (known as the percolation threshold), the probability of finding an infinite cluster rises sharply, moving from near 0 to a substantial value.
> 
> 2. **Ising Model and Phase Transition:**
>    - Similarly, in the Ising model, as temperature decreases (or interaction strength increases), spins tend to align, leading to ferromagnetism. This can be seen as akin to reaching a percolation threshold where spins start to collaborate in forming large, ordered regions (or infinite clusters of aligned spins).
>    - Below a critical temperature (the Curie temperature), the probability of observing an infinitely connected cluster of aligned spins also rises significantly, indicative of a phase transition.
> 
> ### Summary of The Relationship:
> - The statement you referred to about the probability of a node being part of an infinite chunk rising after a critical threshold can be viewed through both percolation theory and the Ising model. Both frameworks exhibit critical behaviors relative to thresholds: 
>   - **In percolation**, the transition happens with the probability of connections,
>   - **In the Ising model**, it occurs with temperature or external fields.
> 
> ### Critical Parameters:
> - In the context of the Ising model, the critical parameter (temperature) and in percolation theory, the critical connection probability, serve analogous roles in determining when the system transitions from a state dominated by finite clusters to one with a significant probability of infinite clusters.
> 
> This connection highlights how different systems can exhibit similar critical behaviors despite appearing fundamentally different at first glance.
